<h2>Export Controls Are Becoming a Central Tool of Economic Policy</h2>

<p>In spring 2026, US policy actions on semiconductor export controls drew widespread attention. Tech Policy Press noted in a deep analysis that &quot;technology restrictions have become a central instrument of economic statecraft&quot; [Tech Policy Press, 2026.04].</p>

<p>CSIS (Center for Strategic and International Studies) analyzed new transatlantic export control dynamics, noting that the US and EU are deepening coordination on export control policy, though differences remain at the implementation level [CSIS, 2026.04].</p>

<p>A notable recent development is the US Congress's proposed MATCH Act — described as a &quot;missing puzzle piece&quot; in the existing export control framework, with a focus on strengthening controls over AI-related technology exports [The Foundation for American Innovation, 2026.04].</p>

<h2>The Actual Impact and Boundaries of Controls</h2>

<h3>Controls Have Had a Material Impact</h3>
<p>Multiple research reports show that US export controls have indeed affected the pace of China's semiconductor development. The South China Morning Post (SCMP) reported that Congress introduced the &quot;largest&quot; upgrade of export controls against China [SCMP, 2026.04].</p>

<h3>But the Boundaries Are Equally Clear</h3>
<p>The American Enterprise Institute (AEI) published an analysis titled &quot;The Lithography Loophole,&quot; noting that China is bypassing key equipment restrictions through alternative technical pathways — such as multiple patterning, self-aligned lithography, and others [AEI, 2026.04]. This demonstrates that controls can slow but not completely prevent technological progress, especially in areas where alternative technical routes have been explored for years.</p>

<p>Reuters also reported in April 2026 that US lawmakers scaled back an initially more aggressive bill targeting Chinese chipmaking [Reuters, 2026.04]. This suggests that domestic industry lobbying and European allies' feedback are placing constraints on the &quot;boundaries&quot; of controls.</p>

<h2>Ripple Effects Across the Industry</h2>

<p>The impact of export controls extends far beyond the US and China:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Global semiconductor equipment supply chain restructuring</strong>: Equipment manufacturers such as Netherlands' ASML and Japan's Tokyo Electron face compliance adjustments for certain high-end equipment exports.</li>
  <li><strong>Accelerated domestic equipment substitution in China</strong>: In certain mid-to-low-end equipment segments, China's localization rate has risen from approximately 20% in 2022 to roughly 40% in 2026 (industry estimates, no official consolidated data [requires further verification]).</li>
  <li><strong>Evolving role of third countries/regions</strong>: Southeast Asia and the Middle East may emerge as new intermediary nodes, further increasing supply chain complexity.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Implications for Business Strategy</h2>

<p>Whether or not a business is directly involved in semiconductors, the shifting export control environment is reshaping the rules of competition in the global tech industry:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Compliance capability as core competitiveness</strong>: Export control compliance is no longer just a legal department concern — it directly affects product planning, supply chain layout, and customer selection.</li>
  <li><strong>Regulatory gray zones of &quot;dual-use&quot; technology</strong>: More general-purpose technologies may fall under control scope. Companies need export classification assessment mechanisms for their technologies.</li>
  <li><strong>Navigating &quot;certainty in the gray zone&quot;</strong>: In the space between control and countermeasures, a company's ability to anticipate policy changes will directly impact business decision quality.</li>
</ul>

